  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory obligated $1.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, across 684 awards with an average award size of $2.1M. The Laboratory’s obligations fell 40.7% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, providing $901M in obligations, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $469M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the dominant market at $1.2B. Historical context shows obligations peaked at $2.4B in FY25 and were $1.5B in FY21, $1.6B in FY22, $2B in FY23, and $2B in FY24. Smaller agency customers include the Department of Homeland Security at $21.8M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $8M. Contracting focus centers on R&D and engineering services, while manufacturing and biotechnology markets account for much smaller shares. Current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the largest agency customers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $901M in FY2026 to date. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $469M. Other notable buyers include the Department of Homeland Security at $21.8M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $8M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What work does the Laboratory sell to the government?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the largest market at $1.2B. Engineering Services accounts for $73.9M and All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services accounts for $55.9M. Smaller markets include Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $7.5M and Research and Development in Nanotechnology at $472K."
      }
    ]
  },
